<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Restoration Spain</h1><p class="st-meta">1874 CE – 1931 CE · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The restored Bourbon monarchy of Alfonso XII and Alfonso XIII, a stable but rigged parliamentary system that lost Spain's last colonies in 1898.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In December 1874 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsenio-martinez-campos">Arsenio Martínez Campos</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-spain">Isabella II</a>, at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saguntum">Sagunto</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a>. The conservative statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-canovas-del-castillo">Antonio Cánovas del Castillo</a> designed the new order and its Constitution of 1876.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the turno pacífico, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-canovas-del-castillo">Cánovas</a>'s Conservatives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praxedes-mateo-sagasta">Sagasta</a>'s Liberals alternated in power through elections managed by local bosses, the caciques. The system kept order but excluded workers and regionalists. Defeat by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1898 cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a>, and the Tragic Week of 1909 and the disaster of Annual in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> in 1921 shook the regime.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera's</a> dictatorship from 1923 to 1930. After republican victories in the municipal elections of April 1931, the king left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> was proclaimed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/restoration-spain.jpg" alt="Restoration Spain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version User:Ignaciogavira ; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1874 CE – 1931 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alfonso XII, Antonio Cánovas del Castillo, Alfonso XIII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Spain (Restoration), Bourbon Restoration</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1902&amp;focus=restoration-spain" aria-label="Restoration Spain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4589 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Restoration Spain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4909 989L4864 1148M4909 989L4655 1514M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L4960 945M4909 989L4910 989M4909 989L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4864 1148h0M4655 1514h0M4909 989h0M4960 945h0M4910 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madrid. <a href="/atlas?year=1902&amp;focus=restoration-spain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
